// The library a program has when it mentions `Thread` and does not declare it itself: a closure started on a thread of its
// own and joined for its result.
//
// A thread shares nothing with the program that starts it: the closure owns what it captured (a closure or a result that
// holds a reference is refused where the copy of spawn is made, MirMoves.checkSpawns), so running it where it is started
// gives the values that running it on another thread gives, and that is how the validator and `tersus eval` read it. A
// caller knows of the result what the closure's bound says, which is nothing: a fact it needs comes through the result's
// type, a struct with an invariant. A Thread is linear, so every thread is joined, once.

linear struct Thread<T> {
    value: T,
};

impl<T> Thread<T> {
    fn spawn<F: FnOnce() -> T>(f: F) -> Self [{ }] [{ }] {
        return Self { value: f() };
    };

    fn join(self: Self) -> T [{ }] [{ affirm return = self.value; }] {
        return self.value;
    };
};
